  "content": "<nav class=\"thesis-breadcrumb\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/projects/\">Projects</a> <span aria-hidden=\"true\">/</span> Undergraduate thesis</nav>\n<section class=\"thesis-hero\" aria-labelledby=\"thesis-title\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Undergraduate thesis · Computational materials science</p><h1 id=\"thesis-title\">First-Principles Investigation of the Structural and Electronic Properties of Two-Dimensional (MoWVNbTa)S₂</h1><p class=\"thesis-lead\">What happens when five transition metals share one two-dimensional lattice? My research uses first-principles calculations to explore the structure and electronic properties of (MoWVNbTa)S₂.</p><p class=\"thesis-authors\">Jan Mariano Dinan S. Mariano<br>With Allan Abraham B. Padama · UPLB</p><ul class=\"thesis-tags\" aria-label=\"Research areas and methods\"><li>Materials science</li><li>Density functional theory</li><li>Quantum ESPRESSO</li></ul></div><figure class=\"thesis-structure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/crystal-structure.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Atomic model of the two-dimensional Mo, W, V, Nb, Ta and sulfur lattice, with its element legend\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/crystal-structure.webp\" width=\"780\" height=\"555\" alt=\"Atomic model of the two-dimensional Mo, W, V, Nb, Ta and sulfur lattice, with its element legend\" loading=\"eager\" decoding=\"async\" fetchpriority=\"high\"></a><figcaption>The atomic model shown in the SPVM poster. The colors distinguish the five metal species and sulfur.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/crystal-structure.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure></section>\n<nav class=\"thesis-jump\" aria-label=\"On this page\"><a href=\"#overview\">Overview</a><a href=\"#methods\">Methods</a><a href=\"#results\">Results</a><a href=\"#presentation\">Presentation</a></nav>\n<section class=\"thesis-section\" id=\"overview\" aria-labelledby=\"overview-title\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">The question</p><h2 id=\"overview-title\">Many elements. One atomic layer.</h2><div class=\"thesis-overview\"><p>High-entropy materials bring several elements into a shared structure. This thesis investigates a two-dimensional transition metal dichalcogenide combining molybdenum, tungsten, vanadium, niobium and tantalum with sulfur.</p><div><h3>From atomic arrangement to electronic behavior</h3><p>The aim is to examine the relaxed structure, follow how electronic bands and states change with energy, and investigate how electron populations differ among local chemical environments.</p><p>The figures below connect these questions across three labeled structural models. Their labels are retained from the research plots; no particular atomic arrangement is inferred from the numbering.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"thesis-section\" id=\"methods\" aria-labelledby=\"methods-title\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Computational approach</p><h2 id=\"methods-title\">From a structural model to an electronic picture.</h2><ol class=\"thesis-methods\"><li><h3>Represent the mixed lattice</h3><p>Special quasirandom structures provide finite-cell models of a disordered arrangement of metal species.</p></li><li><h3>Relax the structure</h3><p>Density functional theory calculations in Quantum ESPRESSO allow atomic positions and the in-plane cell to relax, as described in the poster.</p></li><li><h3>Examine the electronic states</h3><p>Band structures and spin-resolved density-of-states calculations describe the available electronic states. Charge analysis compares electron populations across local environments.</p></li></ol></section>\n<section class=\"thesis-section thesis-results\" id=\"results\" aria-labelledby=\"results-title\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Selected results</p><h2 id=\"results-title\">Four views of the same material.</h2><p class=\"thesis-section-intro\">Start with the selected figures, then expand a comparison to see the other supplied results. Every image links to its original resolution.</p><section class=\"thesis-result\" id=\"bands\" aria-labelledby=\"bands-title\"><div class=\"result-intro\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">01 / Band structures</p><h3 id=\"bands-title\">Following the available electron energies.</h3><p>Band structures show how allowed electron energies change along a path through reciprocal space. Here, the plotted bands cross the Fermi reference. Together with the finite total density of states at that energy, this is consistent with metallic behavior in the calculated models.</p></div><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/band_structure1.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 1 band energies along Gamma–M–K–Gamma with the Fermi level at zero\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/band_structure1.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"1200\" alt=\"Structure 1 band energies along Gamma–M–K–Gamma with the Fermi level at zero\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 1.</strong> Blue curves trace the calculated bands along Γ–M–K–Γ. The red dashed line marks the Fermi level at zero energy; crossings indicate available states at that reference.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/band_structure1.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"result-comparison\"><summary>Compare Structures 2 and 3</summary><div class=\"thesis-gallery\"><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/band_structure2.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 2 band energies along Gamma–M–K–Gamma\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/band_structure2.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"1200\" alt=\"Structure 2 band energies along Gamma–M–K–Gamma\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 2.</strong> The same energy reference and reciprocal-space path make changes in the band positions visible. Bands also intersect the zero-energy reference.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/band_structure2.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/band_structure3.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 3 band energies along Gamma–M–K–Gamma\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/band_structure3.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"1200\" alt=\"Structure 3 band energies along Gamma–M–K–Gamma\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 3.</strong> The same energy reference and reciprocal-space path make changes in the band positions visible. Bands also intersect the zero-energy reference.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/band_structure3.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure></div></details></section><section class=\"thesis-result\" id=\"charge\" aria-labelledby=\"charge-title\"><div class=\"result-intro\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">02 / Charge redistribution</p><h3 id=\"charge-title\">A sulfur atom’s neighbors matter.</h3><p>These comparisons group sulfur sites by the three metals bonded to them. Some groups gain electron population relative to the plotted pristine-sulfur average, while others lose it. The differences highlight the role of the local chemical environment.</p></div><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/ChargeTransferSulfur1.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Sulfur electron-population differences grouped by bonded metal triplets, with positive and negative bars and individual site markers\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/ChargeTransferSulfur1.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"1131\" alt=\"Sulfur electron-population differences grouped by bonded metal triplets, with positive and negative bars and individual site markers\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption>Bars show the mean electron-population difference for each metal-neighbor group; black dots show individual sulfur sites. Blue indicates gain and orange loss relative to the pristine reference. Gray marks the plotted near-zero range.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/ChargeTransferSulfur1.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure><p class=\"thesis-source-note\">The supplied charge-comparison plots do not label their configurations. They are shown as separate analyses, without assigning them to Structures 1–3.</p><details class=\"result-comparison\"><summary>Explore the other sulfur-neighborhood comparisons</summary><div class=\"thesis-gallery\"><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/ChargeTransferSulfur2.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Additional sulfur-neighborhood electron-population comparison B\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/ChargeTransferSulfur2.webp\" width=\"1092\" height=\"1200\" alt=\"Additional sulfur-neighborhood electron-population comparison B\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Additional comparison B.</strong> Gains and losses vary among the labeled metal-neighbor groups. Bars summarize each group and dots retain the site-by-site differences; the configuration identity is not specified in the image.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/ChargeTransferSulfur2.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/ChargeTransferSulfur3.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Additional sulfur-neighborhood electron-population comparison C\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/ChargeTransferSulfur3.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"1131\" alt=\"Additional sulfur-neighborhood electron-population comparison C\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Additional comparison C.</strong> Gains and losses vary among the labeled metal-neighbor groups. Bars summarize each group and dots retain the site-by-site differences; the configuration identity is not specified in the image.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/ChargeTransferSulfur3.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure></div></details></section><section class=\"thesis-result\" id=\"tdos\" aria-labelledby=\"tdos-title\"><div class=\"result-intro\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">03 / Total density of states</p><h3 id=\"tdos-title\">Seeing where electronic states accumulate.</h3><p>The total density of states counts available states at each energy. The peaks show energy ranges with more states, and the nonzero values at the Fermi reference complement the band crossings above.</p></div><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_TDOS1.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 1 total density of states, with spin-up and spin-down curves and a Fermi reference\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/HEA_TDOS1.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"897\" alt=\"Structure 1 total density of states, with spin-up and spin-down curves and a Fermi reference\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 1.</strong> The dashed vertical line marks the Fermi energy. Spin-up and spin-down contributions are plotted on opposite sides of the horizontal axis for comparison; the negative branch is a plotting convention, not a negative number of states.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_TDOS1.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"result-comparison\"><summary>Compare Structures 2 and 3</summary><div class=\"thesis-gallery\"><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_TDOS2.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 2 spin-resolved total density of states\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/HEA_TDOS2.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"897\" alt=\"Structure 2 spin-resolved total density of states\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 2.</strong> The peak shapes vary while both labeled spin channels retain states at the Fermi reference. The two channels are displayed with opposite signs.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_TDOS2.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_TDOS3.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 3 spin-resolved total density of states\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/HEA_TDOS3.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"897\" alt=\"Structure 3 spin-resolved total density of states\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 3.</strong> The peak shapes vary while both labeled spin channels retain states at the Fermi reference. The two channels are displayed with opposite signs.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_TDOS3.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure></div></details></section><section class=\"thesis-result\" id=\"ldos\" aria-labelledby=\"ldos-title\"><div class=\"result-intro\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">04 / Element-resolved states</p><h3 id=\"ldos-title\">Tracing the contributions of each element.</h3><p>Resolving the density of states by element connects the overall electronic picture to its chemical ingredients. Sulfur has prominent contributions below the Fermi reference, while the metal curves contribute across different energy ranges. The states reflect contributions from multiple constituent elements.</p></div><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin1.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 1 element-resolved density of states for Mo, W, V, Nb, Ta and S, with spin channels distinguished by line style\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin1.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"796\" alt=\"Structure 1 element-resolved density of states for Mo, W, V, Nb, Ta and S, with spin channels distinguished by line style\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 1.</strong> Colors distinguish Mo, W, V, Nb, Ta and S. Solid and dashed curves denote the labeled spin-up and spin-down channels; their relative peaks show which elements contribute at different energies.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin1.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"result-comparison\"><summary>Compare Structures 2 and 3</summary><div class=\"thesis-gallery\"><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin2.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 2 element-resolved density of states with labeled spin channels\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin2.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"796\" alt=\"Structure 2 element-resolved density of states with labeled spin channels\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 2.</strong> Sulfur remains prominent below the Fermi reference, alongside smaller metal contributions. The element curves show how contributions vary with energy and between the supplied models.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin2.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"result-figure\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin3.png\" aria-label=\"Enlarge: Structure 3 element-resolved density of states with labeled spin channels\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin3.webp\" width=\"1200\" height=\"796\" alt=\"Structure 3 element-resolved density of states with labeled spin channels\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Structure 3.</strong> Sulfur remains prominent below the Fermi reference, alongside smaller metal contributions. The element curves show how contributions vary with energy and between the supplied models.<a class=\"figure-original\" href=\"/assets/thesis/HEA_elemental_LDOS_spin3.png\">Open full-resolution figure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure></div></details></section></section>\n<section class=\"thesis-section\" id=\"presentation\" aria-labelledby=\"presentation-title\"><div class=\"thesis-presentation\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Research presentation · SPVM 2026</p><h2 id=\"presentation-title\">From computation to conversation.</h2><p>I presented this research at the 28th National Physics Conference of the Samahang Pisika ng Visayas at Mindanao, held at Ateneo de Davao University. The presentation received <strong>Best Presentation in the SPVM Poster Session</strong>.</p><p>The poster brings the computational approach and results together. The conference article shares the experience and the certificate of recognition.</p><div class=\"project-links\"><a class=\"button button-primary\" href=\"/assets/spvm-poster.png\">Open the full poster ↗</a><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"/posts/spvm-28th-national-physics-conference/\">Read the conference article →</a></div></div><figure class=\"thesis-poster\"><a href=\"/assets/spvm-poster.png\"><img src=\"/assets/thesis/web/spvm-poster.webp\" alt=\"SPVM research poster with the computational method, structure, electronic results and conclusions\" width=\"900\" height=\"1272\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption>The original conference poster. <a href=\"/assets/spvm-poster.png\">View full resolution ↗</a></figcaption></figure></div></section>\n"
}
